Looking at the type declarations, the `GeospatialCamera` has a `pitch` property and there's a public `setGeospatialOrientation` function that accepts a `GeospatialOrientation` with all-optional fields. The `GeospatialLimits` include `pitchMin`/`pitchMax` and a `getEffectivePitchMax` that can constrain pitch based on radius. To guarantee the exact pitch is applied regardless of current zoom, I temporarily widen the pitch limits, call the orientation setter with only the pitch field, then restore the limits.

FILE: app/src/globe-tilt.ts
```ts
import type { GeospatialCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import { setGeospatialOrientation } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function tiltGlobeCamera(camera: GeospatialCamera, pitch: number): void {
    const limits = camera.limits;
    const origMin = limits.pitchMin;
    const origMax = limits.pitchMax;

    limits.pitchMin = Math.min(origMin, pitch);
    limits.pitchMax = Math.max(origMax, pitch);

    setGeospatialOrientation(camera, { pitch });

    limits.pitchMin = origMin;
    limits.pitchMax = origMax;
}
```

I wrote `src/globe-tilt.ts` which temporarily widens the camera's pitch limits, calls `setGeospatialOrientation` with only the `pitch` field (so yaw, radius, and center are untouched), then restores the limits. I'm not 100% certain that `setGeospatialOrientation` preserves the other fields when they are omitted — the type marks them optional, which strongly implies it, but I can't verify the runtime behavior without the library. I also can't be fully certain that restoring the limits won't cause a subsequent zoom interaction to re-clamp the pitch via `getEffectivePitchMax`; if that turns out to be a problem in practice, the fix would be to leave the widened limits in place or to re-assert the pitch after each zoom change.
